From: ntpers project <ntpers@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH 2/N] x86: give the NT ABI a distinct audit arch (fail-closed seccomp)

M0 blocker resolved.

PROBLEM

do_syscall_64() calls syscall_enter_from_user_mode(), which drives seccomp,
audit and ptrace using the *Linux* syscall number and AUDIT_ARCH_X86_64.

If NT tasks reuse that path, an SSN is fed into machinery that believes it is
looking at a Linux syscall number. Concretely:

  A container runtime installs the usual seccomp allowlist:
      if (arch == AUDIT_ARCH_X86_64 && nr == __NR_read)  -> ALLOW      (nr 0)
      ...
      default -> KILL_PROCESS

  An NT task issues SSN 0. On Server 2022 SSN 0 is NtAccessCheck, not read().
  The filter sees arch=AUDIT_ARCH_X86_64, nr=0, and ALLOWS it.

The filter author allowlisted read(). They got NtAccessCheck. Every SSN in the
pinned table collides with some Linux number this way, so an NT binary can walk
straight through a policy that was written to contain it. That is a sandbox
escape, not a cosmetic mismatch -- and it is worse than an ordinary bypass
because the filter *appears* to be in force.

Audit has the same defect in a quieter form: audit records would attribute NT
services to unrelated Linux syscall names, silently corrupting the audit trail.

FIX

Report a distinct architecture for NT tasks, so no filter written for
AUDIT_ARCH_X86_64 can ever match an NT service.

Existing filters do not know AUDIT_ARCH_NTX86_64. A correctly written filter
ends in a default deny, so it now denies NT syscalls -- fail-closed, which is
the required direction. A filter ending in a default ALLOW was already
permissive by construction and is not made worse.

We deliberately do NOT invent a fake EM_ value that could collide with a future
upstream allocation. EM_NTX86_64 uses the 0x9000+ private range, and the
selftest asserts it differs from every AUDIT_ARCH_* the kernel defines.

---
 arch/x86/include/asm/syscall.h    | 12 +++++++++++-
 include/uapi/linux/audit.h        |  4 ++++
 arch/x86/entry/nt/enter.c         | 48 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 63 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/include/uapi/linux/audit.h b/include/uapi/linux/audit.h
--- a/include/uapi/linux/audit.h
+++ b/include/uapi/linux/audit.h
@@
 #define EM_X86_64	62
+/*
+ * Private-range machine id for the NT personality. Not an ELF machine type:
+ * NT tasks execute PE images. Placed in the 0x9000+ private range so it can
+ * never collide with an upstream EM_ allocation.
+ */
+#define EM_NTX86_64	0x9040
 
 #define AUDIT_ARCH_X86_64	(EM_X86_64|__AUDIT_ARCH_64BIT|__AUDIT_ARCH_LE)
+#define AUDIT_ARCH_NTX86_64	(EM_NTX86_64|__AUDIT_ARCH_64BIT|__AUDIT_ARCH_LE)

diff --git a/arch/x86/include/asm/syscall.h b/arch/x86/include/asm/syscall.h
--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/syscall.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/syscall.h
@@
 static inline int syscall_get_arch(struct task_struct *task)
 {
+#ifdef CONFIG_NT_PERSONALITY
+	/*
+	 * NT tasks must never be described to seccomp/audit as x86-64, or a
+	 * filter allowlisting Linux nr N will silently allow NT service N.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(test_tsk_thread_flag(task, TIF_NTABI)))
+		return AUDIT_ARCH_NTX86_64;
+#endif
 	/* x32 tasks should be considered AUDIT_ARCH_X86_64. */
 	return (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_IA32_EMULATION) &&
 		task->thread_info.status & TS_COMPAT)
 		? AUDIT_ARCH_I386 : AUDIT_ARCH_X86_64;
 }

--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/x86/entry/nt/enter.c
@@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * NT personality syscall entry work.
+ *
+ * Mirrors syscall_enter_from_user_mode() but keyed to the NT ABI. We reuse
+ * the generic entry helpers rather than reimplementing them, because the
+ * RCU/context-tracking/instrumentation ordering in that path is subtle and
+ * getting it wrong produces bugs that only appear under NO_HZ_FULL.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/entry-common.h>
+#include <linux/seccomp.h>
+#include <linux/audit.h>
+#include <linux/ptrace.h>
+
+#include "../../nt/nt_internal.h"
+
+long nt_syscall_enter(struct pt_regs *regs, unsigned int ssn)
+{
+	unsigned long work = READ_ONCE(current_thread_info()->syscall_work);
+	long ret = (long)ssn;
+
+	enter_from_user_mode(regs);
+	instrumentation_begin();
+
+	if (work & SYSCALL_WORK_ENTER) {
+		/*
+		 * syscall_get_arch() now reports AUDIT_ARCH_NTX86_64, so any
+		 * filter written against AUDIT_ARCH_X86_64 falls through to
+		 * its default action. For a well-formed filter that is a
+		 * deny, which is the behaviour we want.
+		 */
+		ret = syscall_trace_enter(regs, ret, work);
+		if (ret < 0)
+			ret = NT_SYSCALL_SKIP;
+	}
+
+	instrumentation_end();
+	return ret;
+}
+
+void nt_syscall_exit(struct pt_regs *regs)
+{
+	/*
+	 * NTSTATUS is not an errno. The generic exit path applies
+	 * syscall_exit_work()'s error mangling for Linux return values, which
+	 * would corrupt a status like 0xC0000005 into something a PE reads as
+	 * an unrelated failure. We take the audit/ptrace work but keep RAX.
+	 */
+	unsigned long saved_ax = regs->ax;
+
+	syscall_exit_to_user_mode(regs);
+	regs->ax = saved_ax;
+}

---
SELFTEST (tools/testing/selftests/ntpers/seccomp_arch.c), required to pass
before M0 is declared green:

  1. Install a filter: allow AUDIT_ARCH_X86_64 nr 0 (read), default KILL.
     Run an NT task issuing SSN 0. It MUST be killed, not allowed.
     Pre-patch this test fails, which is the proof the bug was real.
  2. AUDIT_ARCH_NTX86_64 != every AUDIT_ARCH_* the kernel defines.
  3. A Linux task under the same filter still runs read() normally
     (no regression to DOD-LINUXR).
  4. Audit records for NT tasks carry arch=NTX86_64 and the NT service name.
